To briefly complete the discussion of terminology and introduce our results, we show the structure of one of our laboratory jets parallel to the wall in Figure 1d. The Mach disk shock is disconnected from the surface, as in the case of the inclined plate (Figure 1c). Almost directly below the Mach disk shock location, a shock wave–boundary layer interaction results in an oblique shock foot that impinges on the plate. Pressure gradients across shock waves interact with the flow along the surface causing upstream thickening of the boundary layer, external flow compression via the oblique shock, and flow (boundary layer) separation on the surface downstream of the oblique shock foot.
We first investigate whether the presence of the parallel surface will change the features of the jet, in particular the location of the Mach disk shock that separates supersonic and subsonic flows. Secondly, we examine the ground signature resulting from the interaction of the jet with the surface and the implications for devastation due to a lateral blast, such as occurred at Mount St. Helens.
